//? Title: Smith-Waterman: actual local scores and traceback
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Two byte sequences may share a high-scoring motif while their flanks disagree.
Local alignment chooses substrings and inserted gaps to maximize a linear-gap
score without forcing the flanks to align. We inspect the actual
dynamic-programming table and selected path, retain AAGT versus AGT and the
embedded GATTACA motif, and compare global alignment explicitly.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

Let H(i,j) be the best local alignment score ending at the two prefixes. Either
extend a diagonal pair, delete from the first input, insert from the second, or
begin anew with zero:

$$H(i,j)=\max\{0,H(i-1,j-1)+s(a_i,b_j),H(i-1,j)+g,H(i,j-1)+g\}.$$
\alttext(The local score is the maximum of zero, a scored diagonal pair, an upward gap extension and a leftward gap extension.)

Induction over increasing row and column proves the recurrence covers the last
operation of every local alignment. A global maximum therefore chooses the
optimal endpoint. Backtracking until a zero chooses its start, discarding
unmatched flanks. The first row-major maximum is retained; traceback ties choose
diagonal, then up, then left. Gap must be nonpositive, with default
match2,mismatch-1,gap-2. There is no affine gap-open penalty.

Both time and allocated table storage are proportional to the input-length
product. The retained table is the actual producing table, not the old report's
reconstruction. Widened candidate sums detect an unrepresentable int score.
Gapped strings contain dash markers; literal dash input bytes are allowed but
the coordinates/path are needed to disambiguate them.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/smith_waterman_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto r = a::smith_waterman_trace("AA", "A");
    std::print("| actual DP row | values including zero border |\n| ---: | --- |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < r.table.size(); ++i)
        std::print("| {} | `{}` |\n", i, r.table[i]);
    std::print("\nSelected rows `{}` and `{}`, score {}, first span [{},{}).\n",
               r.alignment.a_aligned, r.alignment.b_aligned, r.alignment.score,
               r.begin_a, r.end_a);
    g::Diagram concept_diagram(48, 1100);
    concept_diagram.node("n0", {0, 0}, "Local alignment AA versus A");
    concept_diagram.node("n1", {5, 0},
                         std::format("First maximum score {}", r.alignment.score));
    concept_diagram.node("n2", {10, 0},
                         std::format("Actual span [{},{})", r.begin_a, r.end_a));
    concept_diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    concept_diagram.save_svg("build/smith_waterman_tiny.svg");
}
```
